You are helping reimplement (from scratch, in Rust) a JavaScript runtime "lumen" whose compiled binary is /tmp/orig (Node-like CLI: `/tmp/orig file.js`). Its `require('zlib').brotliDecompressSync(buf)` uses a CUSTOM brotli decoder with its own error messages. Your job: build a CATALOG of exactly which error message the original throws for each kind of malformed brotli input (RFC 7932), plus its lax/accepting behaviors, with reproducible test vectors.

HARD RULES: run /tmp/orig only as a black box (no disassembly/decompiling, no reading its bytes, no strace/ltrace, do not print the source of JS functions via toString). No internet. Do not write under /workspace. Keep all your files under /tmp/agent_b/ (copy helper scripts there before modifying them).

WHAT IS KNOWN SO FAR (original's messages): empty input -> Error "brotli: unexpected end of input"; truncated uncompressed meta-block -> "brotli: uncompressed block overruns input"; large-window stream header (WBITS escape 1000 001) -> "brotli: large-window streams are not supported"; input "deadbeef" (hex) decodes successfully to EMPTY output (it starts with ISLAST+ISLASTEMPTY and the decoder ignores the non-zero padding bits and the trailing bytes; node rejects this). Errors are plain `Error` objects whose only meaningful property is .message (no code/errno). Print errors with e.message. console.log prints via String(v), so print buffers as hex.

TOOLS YOU CAN USE:
- /tmp/x/py/bits.py (class BW: put(value, nbits) LSB-first, code(c, n) for MSB-first Huffman codes, align(), bytes()) and /tmp/x/py/brotli_craft.py (helpers: wbits_header(bw, wbits), mlen_header(bw, mlen, islast, uncompressed), simple_trivial_header(...), dict_ref_metablock(...), uncompressed_metablock(bw, data), end_stream(bw), copy_code/ins_code/dist_code/cmd_code, NDBITS) — read them first; they were used to craft valid streams that node accepts. Copy them to /tmp/agent_b/ if you need changes.
- A correct reference brotli decoder (my Rust implementation; correct on valid streams, but ITS ERROR MESSAGES ARE MY PLACEHOLDERS, NOT THE ORIGINAL'S): `/tmp/compress_dev/target/release/compress_dev dec br <file>` and a tracer `/tmp/compress_dev/target/release/compress_dev brtrace <file>` (prints meta-block headers, prefix codes, commands). Node v18 (`node -e`, zlib.brotliCompressSync/brotliDecompressSync) is a reference decoder/encoder too (it reports node's own error codes like ERR__ERROR_FORMAT_PADDING_2, useful to confirm what kind of malformation you produced).

WHAT TO PRODUCE: test vectors for (at least) each of these situations, each run through the original's brotliDecompressSync, recording OK+output hex or the exact error message:
 stream header: every WBITS encoding (10..24, 16, 17, large-window marker, the reserved value), truncated header;
 meta-block header: ISLAST/ISLASTEMPTY combos, MNIBBLES=4/5/6 with a zero last nibble ("exuberant nibble"), metadata meta-block (MNIBBLES escape) with reserved bit set, MSKIPBYTES 0..3, exuberant MSKIPLEN byte, non-zero padding before metadata/uncompressed data, metadata that overruns input, ISLAST with metadata, truncated in each field;
 uncompressed meta-blocks (incl. non-zero padding bits, exact-fit, overrun);
 compressed meta-block header: NBLTYPES encodings (1, 2, 256, values >256 if encodable), block-type/block-count codes, NPOSTFIX/NDIRECT, context modes, NTREES, context map with RLEMAX and IMTF, context map value out of range / RLE run overflowing the map;
 prefix codes: simple codes with NSYM 1..4, symbol >= alphabet size, duplicate symbols, tree_select; complex codes with HSKIP 0/2/3, code-length code whose Kraft sum is not exactly full (over/under), single non-zero code length, repeat code 16/17 behaviors (overflow past alphabet), final space not zero (incomplete / over-subscribed code), all-zero code;
 commands: insert/copy lengths overrunning MLEN ("block length mismatch"-like), distance codes 0..15 producing distance <= 0, distance beyond window/output (dictionary reference) with copy length outside 4..24, dictionary word index/transform id >= 121, transformed word overrunning MLEN, distance referencing before stream start;
 end of stream: missing ISLAST (input ends after a non-last meta-block), trailing garbage after the last meta-block, non-zero padding bits after ISLASTEMPTY; input truncated at many different points of a valid multi-meta-block stream (take a valid stream produced by node's brotliCompressSync for some text at quality 5 and 11 and truncate it at every byte length; record the message for each length — summarize which messages appear and where).
 Also: does the original accept everything node accepts? Run a few hundred valid node-produced streams (various inputs, quality 0..11, lgwin 10..24, modes 0..2) through the original and confirm outputs match.

For each error message found, determine (as precisely as you can by experiment) the exact condition and the order of checks when several problems coexist (e.g. truncated AND invalid: which message wins? is "unexpected end of input" raised as soon as a read passes the end, or are missing bits treated as zero?). Note any check node does that the original skips (e.g. padding bits, Kraft completeness, trailing data).

DELIVERABLES: (1) /tmp/agent_b/cases.json: a JSON array of {"name": str, "hex": str (input bytes), "orig": {"ok": true, "out_hex": str} | {"ok": false, "message": str}} covering all your vectors (keep each input small); (2) your final message: a concise catalog — every distinct error message, its trigger condition, precedence rules, and the lax behaviors — this message is relayed to the implementer (nobody sees your intermediate tool output). Also save it to /tmp/agent_b/REPORT.txt.

